Inspect and reuse the existing Git/dashboard/chat/workflow architecture. Implement all changes end-to-end without breaking existing actions.

1. Dashboard – Targeted Repository Refresh
Currently a Git action on one repo refreshes all repos. Fix this.

For a single-repo Git action:
- refresh only the affected repo if the action can change its state
- do not reload/re-render other repository rows
- do not refresh after read-only actions such as Status, Log/History, Diff, branch info or remote info
- refresh after successful state-changing actions such as Pull, Push, Commit, Checkout/branch switch, Merge, Reset/Revert, Stash Apply/Pop or working-tree changes
- if an action fails without changing state, avoid unnecessary refresh.

Use repo-scoped refresh/update logic rather than refreshAll.

2. “Sync All to Main” Dashboard Action
Add a button immediately left of the “Filter repos” input.

It must sync every available MIM service repo EXCEPT `mim-knowledge-base`.

On click:
- prevent duplicate simultaneous sync runs
- inspect every target repo
- detect current branch and local changes
- safely stash/shelve dirty changes, including relevant untracked files
- assign temporary stashes to the sync operation so they can be tracked safely
- checkout `main`
- pull latest remote `main` using fast-forward-only behavior; never silently merge/rebase
- restore that repo’s previously shelved changes onto the updated main
- continue processing remaining repos even if one repo fails
- never discard local changes.

If a repo has an unfinished merge/rebase/cherry-pick or another unsafe Git state, skip it and mark it Needs Attention rather than modifying it automatically.
If stash restore causes conflicts, preserve the stash/change safely and mark that repo Needs Attention.

Show live console progress throughout:
repo N/total, current repo, stash/checkout/pull/restore steps and result.
Because total repo count is known, show genuine overall progress.
Use toastr lifecycle notifications as implemented in Stage 1.

After the entire operation finishes:
- show summary: synced / skipped / failed / needs-attention repos
- perform ONE dashboard refresh at the end
- all successfully synced repos should be on latest local `main` with their previous local modifications restored.

3. Fix Chat “Create Workflow”
Properly analyze and repair the existing Create Workflow feature.

When clicked:
- use the ENTIRE current chat/session, not only the latest message
- consider transcript, session summary, important decisions/evidence where available
- identify the real current objective
- remove repetition/conversational noise
- exclude rejected or superseded approaches
- prioritize the latest agreed decisions
- preserve important technical details, constraints, service names, commands and safeguards.

Generate a summarized but comprehensive actionable workflow with:
Title
Objective
Prerequisites/Context
Ordered Steps
Decision/Validation Points
Expected Result
Important Constraints/Notes

If the chat contains unrelated topics, include only the relevant workflow context.
Allow review/edit after generation.
Use the existing workflow storage/model if one already exists; do not create a parallel duplicate system.
Creating a workflow must not modify the original chat.

